# Kodo — Architecture Rules

Kodo is an Architecture Control Center for multi-stack codebases (Spring Boot, C#/.NET, Node.js, Python).
This file is binding for every human and AI contributor.

## 1. Core IR is the single source of truth

- The canonical contract lives in `packages/core-ir/schema.json` (JSON Schema 2020-12).
- The same model MUST be implemented identically in three languages:
  - Python: `services/api-python/app/models/ir.py` (Pydantic v2)
  - C#: `services/analyzer-csharp/Models/CoreIr.cs` (records)
  - TypeScript: `apps/web/src/types/core-ir.ts`
- Any change to `WorkspaceIR`, `ComponentNode`, `ComponentRelation` requires updating the schema **and** all three implementations in the same change.
- `WorkspaceIR` is assembled by the orchestrator (`mermaidClassDiagram`, `warnings`, `audit`); `services/analyzer-csharp` only implements `ComponentNode`, `ComponentRelation` and its own request/response envelopes.
- Do not add fields to one implementation only. Do not rename wire fields. Do not invent parallel DTOs that drift from the contract.

## 2. Layer boundaries (no cross-layer leaks)

```
apps/web  ──HTTP/JSON──▶  services/api-python  ──HTTP/JSON──▶  services/analyzer-csharp
(UI only)                 (orchestrator, IR owner)             (Roslyn, C# AST only)
```

- `apps/web` MUST NOT parse source code, infer architecture, or know about Roslyn/Tree-sitter internals.
  It renders the Core IR and reads file text through `GET /api/v1/file`. AI features call only
  `/api/v1/ai/*`; the browser never talks to DeepSeek/Ollama directly.
- `services/api-python` is the only orchestrator. It owns scanning, IR assembly, relation resolution,
  Mermaid generation, warning aggregation, the `/api/v1/dry-run` patch plan, and — for Phase 3c — the
  guarded `/api/v1/apply` + `/api/v1/apply/rollback` write path. It calls the C# analyzer's `/patch`
  endpoint and never returns that text to disk without the apply pre-flight.
- `services/analyzer-csharp` is a pure, stateless AST service. It knows nothing about the frontend,
  Mermaid, or orchestration. It never reads or writes workspace files; it receives file text and
  returns Core IR or proposed text.
- No shared code between services via file imports. Only the JSON contract crosses process boundaries.

### Docker-first packaging (Phase 7 — active)

- Kodo is **Docker-first**. One unified Compose stack (web + Nginx, `api-python`, `analyzer-csharp`)
  is the topology for both local self-hosting and SaaS; local and production differ only in
  configuration. The **Tauri desktop wrapper has been removed** — do not reintroduce desktop shells,
  `@tauri-apps/*` dependencies or desktop-only origins.
- **Phase 8 (SaaS foundation) is active** — multi-tenancy, persistence and auth rules live in §3h.
- **Phase 9 (ephemeral builders) is active** — sandboxed compile/test verification rules live in §3i.
- **Phase 10 (Laya System 1 decision layer, `/v1/systemone`) is active** — rules live in §3j.
- **Phase 11 (local UX, dual-mode folder browser & large-repo ergonomics) is active** — rules live in
  §3k. `GET /api/v1/fs/browse` is zero-copy, local-only and **always 403 in `KODO_MODE=saas`**.
- **Phase 12 (full Python stack support: FastAPI / Django / Flask) is active** — rules live in §3l.
  `tree-sitter-python` drives the analyzer, the byte-offset patcher, the FastAPI generator plugin,
  the Python deploy template and the `kodo-builder-python` image.
- **Phase 13 (production SaaS hardening: Alembic, quotas, TTL cleanup, rate limiting, TLS) is
  active** — rules live in §3m.
- **Phases 1–13 (v0.8.0) are the active delivered contract.** Everything below them is binding:
  do not regress the Core IR, the guarded apply, the sandbox verifier, the advisory System 1 lane,
  the local-only folder browser, the Python stack or the SaaS migrations/quotas/rate limits.
  The v0.6+ roadmap (Phases 11–13) is **complete**.
- **Phases 14–15 (v0.9+) are planned only — not active for implementation.** Structured layered
  UML/Graph layout with SVG/PNG image export (Phase 14) and the Kodo MCP Server (Phase 15) are recorded
  in [ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md) as the next evolution track. Their unchecked sub-tasks and Definitions of
  Done are **design intent, not a contract**: do not scaffold `/mcp` endpoints or MCP tools, export UI,
  layout rewrites, a `stdio` bridge or any dependency for them until a future session explicitly
  activates the phase (update §2 here and mark the roadmap task in progress at that point). When
  activated they must **extend** — never bypass — the §3 write policy and guarded apply, §3h auth and
  sandbox containment, §3j advisory System 1 rules and §3m SaaS guards/rate limits.

## 3. Write policy (hard rule)

Phases 1 (visualizer), 2 (pattern audit), 3a (draft editing), 3b (dry-run AST patching) and the
generator half of Phase 4 (blueprint rendering) are **100% zero-write** with respect to user source
code. Phase 3c adds exactly one, tightly guarded write path (see §3c), and Phase 4 reuses it for
create-only scaffolding (§3d). Phase 5 adds AI **proposals** that must come back through that same
reviewed write path (§3e). Phase 6 adds container artifact generation (§3f) that reuses the same
pipeline, plus local Docker/Git operations that never write workspace files except through the guarded
apply. Anything else stays read-only.

Forbidden:
- Writing, patching, moving, or deleting user source files outside the guarded apply service.
- Automatic apply / "fix it for me" disk mutations without an explicit user confirmation.
- Overwriting an existing file while scaffolding — a create-only patch that targets an existing path
  must fail with HTTP 409 `FILE_EXISTS`.
- Mutating Git history or remote refs outside the explicit `/api/v1/git/export` user action
  (branch, commit, optional push); never `reset --hard`, `clean -fd`, force-push or rewrite history.
- Any LLM call that writes to disk, applies its own patch, or bypasses the Monaco diff/apply review.
- Running Docker or Git with `shell=True`, an unvalidated `cwd`, or interpolated user input.
- Persisting a Git/GitHub token in `.git/config`, a remote URL, the audit log, an error message, a
  response body or any file on disk.

Allowed:
- Reading source text from a root that the user explicitly provides.
- Parsing it in memory and returning Core IR.
- Running the deterministic audit engine over the Core IR and returning `WorkspaceIR.audit`.
- Applying AST rewrite operations to **in-memory** syntax trees/text (Tree-sitter byte offsets for
  Java/TS/JS/**Python**, Roslyn syntax rewriting for C#) and returning `originalContent` /
  `proposedContent` through `POST /api/v1/dry-run`. The returned text is never written by the dry-run
  itself.
- Rendering stack-plugin templates into **in-memory** file text (`app/generators/`) and returning it
  through `POST /api/v1/scaffold/dry-run` as create-only patches. Nothing is written there either.
- Rendering Docker artifacts into **in-memory** file text (`app/deploy/generator.py`) and returning it
  through `POST /api/v1/deploy/dry-run` (§3f).
- Reading `kodo.blueprint.json` from the workspace root (`GET /api/v1/blueprint`).
- Calling an OpenAI-compatible LLM (`app/llm/`) with compact Core IR context and a bounded set of
  source files, returning proposals as dry-run patches or a validated blueprint (§3e). The proposal
  text is never written by the AI module.
- Inspecting Git status read-only through `app/services/git_safety.py` (`GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`, no
  mutating subcommands).
- Writing reviewed patches through `app/services/apply_service.py` only (§3c, §3d).
- Running `docker compose` inspection/up/down through `app/deploy/runner.py` (§3f) and
  cloning/exporting through `app/services/git_remote.py` (§3f).
- Streaming an **in-memory** tar snapshot into a disposable, non-root, network-isolated builder
  container through `app/builders/runner.py` (§3i) — the workspace is never mounted or written.
- Serving cached in-memory analysis results and rollback snapshots to the UI.

## 3a. Audit engine rules

- Every audit rule is deterministic, pure and derives from `WorkspaceIR` nodes/relations only.
  No filesystem access, no network, no clock, no randomness.
- Rules are registered through `app.audit.engine.audit_rule` and live in `app/audit/rules/`.
  Adding a rule must not require touching the orchestrator.
- Finding ids are stable (`RULE_ID:target`) and findings are emitted in a deterministic order
  (severity, ruleId, id) so two scans of the same tree produce byte-identical reports.
- The score is a fixed penalty per finding severity (error 10, warning 4, info 1), clamped to 0–100.
- Findings name the exact components involved and always include a concrete, human-applied suggestion.

## 3b. Dry-run patching rules

- The browser's only patching entry point is `POST /api/v1/dry-run` on the orchestrator. The UI must
  not call Roslyn directly or know Tree-sitter/Roslyn internals.
- `services/analyzer-csharp` exposes `POST /patch` for the orchestrator only. It is stateless:
  `{ filePath, content, operations }` in, `{ proposedContent, appliedOps, warnings }` out. No
  filesystem access, no writes.
- Java and TS/JS patchers must make surgical byte-offset edits inside the target type body and
  preserve every untouched token, method, comment and line ending. Regenerating whole files is
  forbidden.
- The Python patcher (`app/patching/python_patcher.py`) edits class attributes and decorators by
  byte offset, keeps class/module docstrings and hand-written methods verbatim, and only inserts
  imports it knows (`Field`, `mapped_column`, `relationship`, `dataclass`, …). A module-level
  FastAPI/Flask router has no editable attributes: report a warning and leave the file untouched.
- The C# patcher must use `CSharpSyntaxTree.ParseText` + trivia-preserving rewriting. Methods,
  comments and formatting outside the targeted member/attribute stay verbatim.
- Every patcher degrades gracefully: unsupported constructs produce a warning and leave the file's
  proposed content byte-identical; they never raise a 500 and never write.
- Patching is bounded like inspection: strict path containment, 2 MB per file, and a bounded number
  of operations per request.

## 3c. Safe-write rules (apply to disk)

- **Every disk write goes through `services/api-python/app/services/apply_service.py`.** No route,
  service, analyzer or patcher may call `open(..., "w")`, `Path.write_text`, `Path.write_bytes`,
  `shutil` or `os.replace` on workspace files. The only writer is `_atomic_write()`.
- Entry point is `POST /api/v1/apply` (and `POST /api/v1/apply/rollback`). Both are synchronous
  FastAPI handlers so blocking Git/file I/O runs on the threadpool, and both take a module lock so
  applies and rollbacks never interleave.
- Pre-flight happens **before any byte is written**, in this order:
  1. Resolve each `filePath` and reject absolute paths, `..`, symlink escapes, non-files and files
     over the 2 MB cap (`PATH_REJECTED`, `FILE_TOO_LARGE`, `NOT_UTF8`).
  2. Inspect Git status read-only. Dirty tree without `allowDirty` → HTTP 409 `GIT_DIRTY` (with
     `dirtyFiles`). Not a repo / no `git` binary without `allowNonGit` → HTTP 409 `NOT_A_GIT_REPO`.
  3. Verify every file's current SHA-256 against the `expectedSha256` the dry-run returned. Any
     mismatch aborts the **entire** batch with HTTP 409 `STALE_FILE` (with `paths`). Partial applies
     are forbidden.
- Dry-run responses must include `originalSha256` (SHA-256 of the exact bytes read from disk), and the
  UI must echo it back untouched. Never re-hash a stale string client-side.
- Writes are atomic: sibling temp file → `fsync` → `os.replace`, bytes written as UTF-8 **without**
  newline translation (the patchers preserve the file's own CRLF/LF style). A failure mid-batch
  restores every file already written in that batch.
- Keep an in-memory rollback snapshot (`applyId`, `rootPath`, timestamp, `{filePath, previousContent,
  writtenSha256}`) bounded to the last 10 batches. `apply/rollback` verifies the files still match
  `writtenSha256` before restoring and aborts the whole rollback on a mismatch unless `force: true`.
  Batches are single-use and lost on restart; the UI must say so before a non-Git apply.
- Whenever a file is applied or rolled back, the UI clears the draft and re-runs `POST /api/v1/scan`
  so the graph, UML and health score reflect disk state immediately.
- Never add "apply on save", auto-apply or watch-mode writes. A human must press the Apply button and
  acknowledge any dirty/non-Git override explicitly.

## 3d. Blueprint & scaffolding rules

- The blueprint contract is versioned (`schemaVersion: "1.0"`). A change to it must update
  `packages/core-ir/blueprint.schema.json`, `app/models/blueprint.py` and
  `apps/web/src/types/blueprint.ts` in the same change. Unknown versions are rejected, not guessed.
- Generator plugins (`app/generators/`) are pure: `blueprint -> list[GeneratedFile]`. No filesystem,
  no network, no clock, no randomness. Templates live in `app/generators/templates/<stack>/`.
- Generated code must be architecturally sound by construction: controllers touch DTOs and services
  only (never entities or repositories), services use constructor injection, entities stay inside the
  persistence boundary. The self-audit bar is **100/100 (0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 info)** on Kodo's own
  audit engine; `tests/test_generators.py` enforces it for every stack.
- `POST /api/v1/scaffold/dry-run` returns the normal `DryRunResponse`; generated files are
  `isNewFile: true` with `originalContent: ""` and `originalSha256: null`. The dry-run detects
  conflicts and flags them, but never writes.
- Apply pre-flight for `isNewFile`: strict containment via `resolve_new_member` (no absolute paths, no
  `..`, no symlink folders), and the target must not exist — a single conflict aborts the **whole**
  batch with HTTP 409 `FILE_EXISTS` (`paths`). Parent folders are created one level at a time inside
  the root; rollback deletes the created files (verifying `writtenSha256` unless `force: true`) and
  prunes empty folders the batch created, never `rootPath`.
- `kodo.blueprint.json` is the only path that may be updated by a scaffold batch, and only through the
  ordinary SHA-256 overwrite path (`isNewFile: false`, `expectedSha256` echoed from the dry-run).

## 3e. AI Copilot rules (DeepSeek Cloud / local Ollama / vLLM)

- **Zero silent AI writes.** The LLM never touches the workspace. `app/llm/` may read at most three
  files per workflow, and every result is a standard `DryRunResponse` (or a validated `Blueprint` for
  the Builder) that a human reviews in the Monaco diff and applies via `POST /api/v1/apply`. Never add
  an auto-fix, "apply on generate", watch mode or background AI write.
- **IR context packing, never source dumps.** Prompts are built from `app/llm/context_packer.py`:
  stacks, health, audit findings, field/method signatures and relations projected into a deterministic
  document bounded by `KODO_LLM_MAX_CONTEXT_CHARS` (default 24 000, truncation flagged). Never paste a
  directory tree, `node_modules`, or unrelated files into a prompt.
- **Bounded, contained reads.** `audit-fix` and `implement-method` read through
  `app/patching/paths.resolve_member` (no absolute paths, no `..`, no symlink escapes) under the 2 MB
  cap and at most three files. Path traversal must keep returning HTTP 400.
- **Strict output validation.** Blueprint JSON must validate against the Phase 4 Pydantic `Blueprint`
  (exactly one automatic repair retry, then 422 with field-level details). Patch edits may only target
  the files Kodo provided; anything else is 422 `INVALID_PATCH`. Model-supplied paths are untrusted and
  are never resolved directly.
- **No secrets anywhere.** API keys come from `KODO_LLM_API_KEY` or the per-request `llmConfig` only.
  Never log them, never echo them in errors, never persist them server-side, never write them into the
  target repository, never commit them. The UI keeps them in `sessionStorage`/memory only.
- **Deterministic tests.** `tests/test_ai_copilot.py` injects an `httpx.MockTransport` through
  `app.llm.client.set_transport_override`; CI must never require network access or an API key.
- **Air-gapped parity.** Every workflow must work against a local OpenAI-compatible server with an
  empty API key; requiring a key for loopback/private hosts is a bug.
- **Structured errors.** Return `{ code, message }` payloads: `MISSING_API_KEY` / `UNAUTHORIZED` (401),
  `RATE_LIMITED` (429), `TIMEOUT` (504), `UNREACHABLE` (502), `INVALID_JSON` / `INVALID_BLUEPRINT` /
  `INVALID_PATCH` (422). Messages must be safe to display and free of prompt content and secrets.

## 3f. Deploy Studio & Git adapter rules (Docker + SaaS)

- **Same guarded write path.** `app/deploy/generator.py` is pure text-in/text-out. Docker artifacts
  (`Dockerfile`, `Dockerfile.sidecar`, `.dockerignore`, `docker-compose.yml`, `.env.example`) are
  returned by `POST /api/v1/deploy/dry-run` as a normal `DryRunResponse`; they reach disk only through
  `POST /api/v1/apply` and are undone by `POST /api/v1/apply/rollback`. Never write them from the
  generator, the runner or a route.
- **Create-only by default.** Existing container files are create-only patches (`409 FILE_EXISTS`)
  unless the caller sets `overwriteExisting: true`, in which case they are ordinary SHA-256 verified
  overwrites (`originalSha256` echoed by the UI). Never silently replace a user's Dockerfile.
- **Image rules.** Every generated Dockerfile is multi-stage, pins an Alpine/slim runtime, creates a
  `kodo` user/group and drops privileges (`USER kodo`), exposes the configured port and declares a
  `HEALTHCHECK`. Spring Boot must use `eclipse-temurin:21-jre-alpine` with `/actuator/health`;
  Python uses `python:3.12-slim` with `uvicorn app.main:app` and a `urllib` healthcheck.
- **WPF honesty.** WPF is Windows-only. For `csharp-wpf` emit a clear warning, no Linux `Dockerfile`,
  and an optional sidecar/API template (`Dockerfile.sidecar` + `profiles: ["sidecar"]`). Never claim a
  desktop UI can be containerized on Linux.
- **Secrets in templates are placeholders.** `.env.example` may only contain obvious placeholders
  (`change-me-local-only`) and compose values use `${VAR}` interpolation. Never emit a real credential,
  token or production password — and never copy a value out of the analysed repository.
- **Subprocess safety.** Docker and Git run through `app/deploy/runner.py` / `app/services/git_remote.py`
  with list arguments, `shell=False`, an explicit timeout, and `cwd` set to a `resolve_root`-validated
  folder (or Kodo's own workspaces dir). No `shell=True`, no string interpolation into a command, no
  unbounded process. Docker commands only run when `docker-compose.yml` exists inside that root.
- **Input sanitization.** Service names must match `[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.-]{0,62}`; log `tail` is
  clamped to 1–1000; CLI output is ANSI-stripped and length-clipped; only `ps`, `version`, `info`,
  `logs`, `compose up` and `compose down` are ever executed.
- **Git URL policy.** Clone accepts `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>` or `https://gitlab.com/<owner>/<repo>`
  only (plus `KODO_GIT_ALLOWED_HOSTS`). Reject `file://`, `ssh`, embedded credentials, query strings,
  traversal, spaces and shell metacharacters with `400 INVALID_REPO_URL`.
- **Tokens are transient.** Pass Git tokens only as `-c http.extraHeader=Authorization: Basic …` for a
  single invocation; keep them in UI memory/sessionStorage only. Never put them in a remote URL, never
  write them to `.git/config`, never log or audit them, never return them in a response, and never
  include them in an error message.
- **Clone destination.** Shallow clones (`--depth 1 --single-branch`) land in
  `KODO_WORKSPACES_DIR` (default `~/.kodo/workspaces/<slug>-<shortid>`), never inside the Kodo repo or
  the currently scanned root.
- **Export policy.** Validate branch names (`[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._/-]{1,199}`, no `..`, `@{`,
  `refs/`, trailing dot/slash/`.lock`) before any Git call. Branch + commit are allowed; push and PR
  creation only happen with `createPr`, a GitHub `origin` and a session token. Never force-push,
  reset, clean or rewrite history.
- **Audit trail.** `app/services/audit_log.py` records every `apply`, `rollback`, `deploy_up`,
  `deploy_down`, `git_clone` and `git_export` in the per-user `audit_events` table (local mode also
  mirrors one JSONL line to `~/.kodo/audit.jsonl` / `KODO_AUDIT_LOG_PATH`), outside the repository.
  Metadata only — never file contents, never tokens, with credential-shaped fragments redacted.
  Auditing is best-effort and must never break the action it is recording.
- **Deterministic tests.** `tests/test_deploy_studio.py` mocks the Docker CLI (`_run_cli`) and the
  GitHub API (`httpx.MockTransport` via `set_transport_override`); `tests/test_saas_foundation.py`
  mocks GitHub OAuth the same way. pytest must never need a Docker daemon, database server or
  internet access; `tests/conftest.py` redirects the audit log and database to `tmp_path`.

## 3g. Docker-first packaging & CI rules (Phase 7)

- **One stack, two profiles.** `docker-compose.yml` runs `web` (Nginx on 8080), `api-python` (8088)
  and `analyzer-csharp` (5050) on the `kodo-net` bridge with healthchecks and
  `depends_on: condition: service_healthy`. `docker-compose.prod.yml` only swaps local builds for
  GHCR images and stops publishing internal ports. Never fork a second production topology.
- **Images are multi-stage, minimal and non-root.** `api-python` builds into `/opt/venv` on
  `python:3.12-slim` and runs as `kodo` (uid 10001) with `/data/kodo` writable and `git` available;
  `web` builds with `node:22-alpine` and serves from `nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged` as `nginx`;
  `analyzer-csharp` keeps the `aspnet:8.0-alpine` non-root runtime. No SDKs, build tools or root
  runtime in the final stage.
- **Same-origin by default.** Production bundles call the API through relative paths (`/api/v1/...`,
  `/health`) proxied by Nginx; `VITE_API_BASE_URL` exists only for split deployments. Do not
  reintroduce desktop-shell origins or wildcard CORS.
- **Explicit state only.** The single persistent volume is `kodo-data:/data/kodo`; host scanning is
  the opt-in `${KODO_LOCAL_PROJECTS_DIR:-./.kodo-empty}` → `/projects` bind mount. Never bake host
  paths, credentials or `.env` values into an image.
- **Secrets stay runtime-only.** `KODO_LLM_*` values come from `.env`/environment; `.env.example`
  stays placeholder-only and `.env` stays git-ignored.
- **CI is the merge gate.** `.github/workflows/ci.yml` must stay green for `pytest`, `ruff check`,
  `dotnet build` and `npm run build`, and build all three Dockerfiles on every PR.
  `.github/workflows/docker-publish.yml` publishes semver + `latest` + sha tags to GHCR on `v*` tags
  only, from `linux/amd64`, with lowercase owner names and `secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN`.
- **Local dev stays supported.** `uvicorn app.main:app --reload` + `npm run dev` against the published
  `:5050` analyzer must keep working; Docker is the primary workflow, not the only one.

## 3h. Multi-tenancy, persistence & auth rules (Phase 8 — active)

- **Two modes, one codebase.** `KODO_MODE=local` must stay zero-friction: no GitHub credentials, a
  seeded `local` user, SQLite under `KODO_DATA_DIR`/`~/.kodo` (or `/data/kodo` in Docker) and all
  pre-Phase-8 behaviour (including the JSONL audit mirror) intact. `KODO_MODE=saas` is fail-closed.
- **Database is configuration, not code.** `KODO_DATABASE_URL` accepts SQLite and any PostgreSQL URL
  (bundled `--profile saas` service or a managed third party). Never hard-code a DSN, never require a
  specific provider, and never commit credentials. Schema changes go through **Alembic** (see §3m);
  `create_all` only remains as the `KODO_DB_AUTO_MIGRATE=false` fallback.
- **Every workspace path is gated.** Routes that accept a root path must call
  `enforce_workspace_access(root, user)`: local mode resolves the folder, SaaS mode additionally
  requires it to resolve inside `<KODO_WORKSPACES_DIR>/<user_id>/` (403 `SAAS_PATH_FORBIDDEN`).
  Clones land in the user sandbox; deletion may only remove a directory inside it.
- **Sessions are JWTs in HttpOnly cookies.** HS256 via `app/auth/jwt_utils.py`; `KODO_JWT_SECRET`
  (≥32 chars) is mandatory in SaaS mode and validated at startup. Also accept
  `Authorization: Bearer <token>` for API clients. Never log or echo tokens, OAuth secrets or codes.
- **Tenant data is always scoped.** Rollback snapshots, audit events, workspaces, blueprints and
  settings are read/written with the authenticated `user_id`. Returning another tenant's `applyId`
  must be indistinguishable from an unknown one (`404 UNKNOWN_APPLY`).
- **Secrets never persist.** `user_settings` stores provider/model preferences only; any
  credential-shaped key (`apiKey`, `token`, `secret`, `password`) is rejected with
  `400 SECRET_NOT_ALLOWED`. LLM API keys and GitHub tokens stay in the request/browser.
- **SaaS disables host control.** `/api/v1/deploy/docker/*` returns
  `403 DOCKER_CONTROL_DISABLED_IN_SAAS`; `/deploy/dry-run` and Git export remain enabled.
- **Hermetic tests.** `tests/conftest.py` points `KODO_DATABASE_URL` at a per-test temp SQLite file and
  resets the engine; GitHub OAuth uses `httpx.MockTransport`. No test may need a real database server,
  Docker daemon, or network access.

## 3i. Ephemeral builder sandbox rules (Phase 9 — active)

- **Zero workspace mutation.** `Verify Build` never writes to the workspace. `app/builders/staging.py`
  walks the root, skips `.git`, `node_modules`, `target`, `bin`, `obj`, `.venv`, `__pycache__`, drops
  symlinks escaping the root, caps the snapshot at 50 MB and overlays proposed patches **in memory**
  (`resolve_member` / `resolve_new_member`). The container receives a tar stream via `docker cp -`; the
  host folder is never bind-mounted into a builder.
- **Hardened flags are not optional.** Every builder container is created with `--network=<mode>`
  (default `none`), `--cap-drop=ALL`, `--security-opt=no-new-privileges:true`, `--memory`, `--cpus`,
  `--pids-limit=256`, `--user 10001:10001` and `-w /workspace`. Never add `--privileged`, host mounts,
  environment passthrough or an interactive shell.
- **Allow-listed stacks and images only.** `java`, `csharp`, `nodejs` and `python` map to
  `KODO_BUILDER_IMAGE_*`; never accept an image, command or entrypoint from the client. Commands are
  fixed constants; the only `sh -c` payloads (`tsc --noEmit[ && vitest run]`, the dotnet
  restore+build/test pair and `ruff check … && mypy …[ && pytest -q]`) are constants executed inside
  the container, never interpolated user input, and every subprocess uses `shell=False` on the host.
- **Timeout + guaranteed cleanup.** `KODO_BUILDER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` (default 120) bounds the run; the
  client is killed and `docker rm -f <id>` always runs in a `finally` block, including create/copy
  failures and timeouts. A missing daemon or image is a reported `status: "unavailable"`, never a 500.
- **Tenant gate + audit.** Both verify routes call `enforce_workspace_access(root, user)` and record a
  `verify_build` event through `app/services/audit_log.py` (metadata only, no logs or file contents).
  SaaS keeps host Docker control disabled; builders also default to disabled there
  (`KODO_BUILDERS_ENABLED=false`) unless an operator opts in.
- **AI fix loop is proposal-only.** `/api/v1/ai/fix-build-errors` reads at most three files (in-flight
  patch content preferred), sends bounded diagnostics plus an ANSI-free log excerpt, and returns a
  standard `DryRunResponse` whose `originalContent`/`originalSha256` stay the disk truth. It never
  writes; the fix still goes through the Monaco diff and the guarded apply.
- **Deterministic tests.** `tests/test_builder_verify.py` mocks the Docker CLI (`runner._run_cli`,
  `_stream_container`) and the LLM (`httpx.MockTransport`). pytest must never require a Docker daemon,
  network access or a host compiler.

## 3j. System 1 / Laya advisory rules (Phase 10 — active)

- **Advisory, never the sole gate.** `app/decision/` may triage findings, recommend a route and
  pre-screen a diff, but it must never write to disk and never replace the deterministic Audit Engine,
  the Monaco Diff review, the SHA-256 guarded apply or the Phase 9 builder verification. No
  "auto-apply on confident triage", no hidden background calls.
- **Wire protocol discipline.** Talk to `POST /v1/systemone` with `{ model, state, questions }` and
  parse `{ model, answers, usage }` defensively (optional `abstain`, `route`, `prediction_set`). Serialize
  requests with an explicit key order — never `sort_keys=True` — clamp `state` to **12 KiB** and the
  whole body to **64 KiB**. Every `choice` question stays <= 12 options with an explicit neutral
  fallback (`uncertain` / `human_review` / `careful_manual_review`); question ids and option keys are
  stable API surface.
- **No text generation.** Laya answers typed questions only. Never ask it for prose, code or free-form
  JSON, and never feed its answer text into a prompt as instructions.
- **Deterministic gating.** Below `KODO_SYSTEMONE_AUTO_THRESHOLD` (default `0.78`) or on `abstain`,
  Kodo escalates to `deepseek_reasoning`/`human_review` instead of acting. Keep the escalation mapping
  deterministic and covered by tests; never let a user-supplied threshold bypass a safety gate.
- **Graceful degradation is part of the contract.** Disabled/unreachable/timeout/invalid responses must
  return HTTP 200 with `available: false` (or a safe `human_review` recommendation) and a
  `SYSTEMONE_*` warning. Scans, audits, dry-runs, applies and builds must never fail because Laya is
  offline.
- **Tenant-scoped ADRs only.** Every `architecture_decision_records` read/write goes through
  `repository.*` with the authenticated `user_id`; another tenant's ADR is a `404`/empty list. Feedback
  records a `decision_feedback` audit event (metadata only) and the training export contains only the
  current user's confirmed/overridden rows.
- **No secrets anywhere.** `KODO_SYSTEMONE_API_KEY` or the per-request `systemOneConfig.apiKey` are
  attached to the outgoing request only. Never log them, echo them in errors, persist them in ADRs or
  the database, or write them into a workspace. The UI keeps them in `sessionStorage`/memory only.
- **Deterministic tests.** `tests/test_decision_layer.py` injects an `httpx.MockTransport` through
  `app.decision.client.set_transport_override`; CI must never require Ollama, `laya-serve`, a Docker
  daemon or network access.

## 3k. Local-only folder browser & graph ergonomics rules (Phase 11 — active)

- **Zero-copy, in place, read-only.** `GET /api/v1/fs/browse` (`app/services/fs_browser.py`,
  `app/api/fs_routes.py`) lists **one** directory level with `scandir` and marker filenames only. It
  never reads file contents, never uploads, copies, moves or writes anything, and never becomes a scan
  shortcut: the UI fills the path input and calls the ordinary `POST /api/v1/scan`.
- **SaaS is always locked out.** `KODO_MODE=saas` must answer
  `403 { "code": "FS_BROWSE_DISABLED_IN_SAAS" }` for every browse request — enforced in the
  `tenant_guard` middleware **before** authentication (so an anonymous SaaS request never sees `401`)
  and re-checked in the route. The Browse button is never rendered in SaaS mode.
- **Roots.** `KODO_BROWSE_ROOTS` (comma-separated, resolved, existing folders) is the explicit
  allow-list. When it is empty: Docker local mode (`/.dockerenv` or the `/projects` mount) uses
  `/projects` plus `KODO_WORKSPACES_DIR`; native mode exposes Windows drive roots or `~` + `/`. Never
  add a root implicitly from a client-supplied value, and never accept a client-supplied root list.
- **Strict containment when configured or in Docker.** Reject any requested path (or symlink target)
  outside the allowed roots with `403 BROWSE_PATH_OUTSIDE_ROOTS`; `resolve()` first so `..` and symlink
  escapes are rejected the same way; `parentPath` is `null` at a root boundary. A symlink that escapes
  a root is never listed.
- **Degradation, never crashing.** Unreadable children, broken symlinks and unreadable marker files are
  skipped (no 500, no exception); an unreadable *current* folder is `403 BROWSE_PERMISSION_DENIED`.
  Missing configured roots are `409 BROWSE_NO_ROOTS` with a setup message.
- **Noise + bounds.** Hidden/system/heavy folders (`.git`, `node_modules`, `target`, `bin`, `obj`,
  `.venv`, `venv`, `__pycache__`, `.idea`, `.vscode`, `$RECYCLE.BIN`, `System Volume Information`, and
  any dot-folder) stay hidden unless `showHidden=true`; listings are sorted and capped at **500**
  entries with `truncated: true`. Keep marker detection immediate-children only — no recursion.
- **Empty Docker mount is actionable, not silent.** Browsing `/projects` with no real subdirectory
  returns `emptyMountHint: true` + a `hintMessage` naming `KODO_LOCAL_PROJECTS_DIR` and
  `docker compose up -d`. Keep both fields in the response contract.
- **Filtering is client-side and non-destructive.** `lib/graphFilter.ts` / `useGraphFilter.ts` derive a
  *view* of the Core IR for the graph and UML only. The draft editor, the audit re-score and the guarded
  apply must keep operating on the full IR; never persist or apply a filtered subset. 1-hop focus is
  depth 1 (selected + direct in/out neighbours + edges between them) and must be inert without a
  selection.
- **UML subsets stay server-side.** The filtered/1-hop diagram comes from the stateless
  `POST /api/v1/mermaid` (`app/services/mermaid.py` reuse, no disk access, no new writes). Do not
  re-implement Mermaid generation in `apps/web`.
- **Deterministic tests.** `tests/test_fs_browser.py` uses temporary roots and monkeypatches the Docker
  mount / runtime detection; no test may browse a real host folder, need a Docker daemon or touch the
  network.

## 3l. Python stack rules (Phase 12 — active)

- **One analyzer, Tree-sitter only.** `app/analyzers/python_analyzer.py` is pure: text in, Core IR
  out. It never imports or executes project code and never touches the filesystem. Import resolution
  lives in `python_imports.py`; layer/kind classification in `python_layers.py`.
- **Detection and noise.** `pyproject.toml`, `requirements.txt`, `setup.py`/`setup.cfg` or any `.py`
  source marks the workspace as `python`. Skip `.venv`, `venv`, `__pycache__`, `.pytest_cache`,
  `.mypy_cache`, `.ruff_cache`, `build`, `dist`, `*.egg-info` and `site-packages`. Test files
  (`tests/`, `test_*.py`, `*_test.py`, `conftest.py`) never enter the graph, so they cannot create
  layer or orphan findings.
- **IR mapping.** Classes become `class`/`enum`/`interface` nodes; module-level `APIRouter`,
  `FastAPI` and `Blueprint` route modules become synthetic `router` nodes (layer `controller`, id =
  relative file path, methods = route handlers). Pydantic DTOs use the `other` layer because the
  canonical contract has no `dto` layer; `MISSING_VALIDATION` still recognises them by name and by
  `Field(...)`/`@field_validator` markers.
- **Relations.** `from … import …` / `import …` resolve to workspace files (package-root aware file
  hints); class attributes and `__init__`/`Depends(...)` parameters become `depends_on`; base
  classes become `inherits`/`implements`; `relationship(...)` targets become `references`. Never
  guess an ambiguous simple name.
- **Audit stays deterministic.** Python gets no separate rule engine: router-to-repository leakage is
  `LAYER_VIOLATION`, circular imports are `CIRCULAR_DEPENDENCY`, god classes are `GOD_CLASS` and
  Pydantic payloads without constraints are `MISSING_VALIDATION`. Findings must be byte-identical
  for identical trees.
- **Patching is proposal-only.** `patch_python` returns text through `/api/v1/dry-run`; disk writes
  still require the Monaco diff + SHA-256 guarded apply. Docstrings, methods, comments and line
  endings outside the edited attribute stay verbatim, and only known imports are inserted.
- **Generator.** `python-fastapi` renders SQLAlchemy model → Pydantic request/response → repository →
  service → `APIRouter` (+ pytest skeleton) and a `pyproject.toml` / `app.main:app` skeleton.
  Generated projects must self-audit at **100/100** with Kodo's own Python analyzer.
- **Deploy + builder.** The Python Dockerfile is multi-stage (`python:3.12-slim`), runs as uid 10001
  `kodo`, exposes port 8000 and healthchecks `/health` through `urllib` (slim has no curl). The
  `kodo-builder-python` image is pre-warmed with ruff/mypy/pytest and verifies with `--network=none`
  and the fixed `ruff check` / `mypy` / `pytest` commands.
- **Deterministic tests.** `tests/test_python_stack.py` covers the analyzer, patcher, generator,
  deploy template and builder configuration without network access, a Docker daemon or a host
  toolchain.

## 3m. SaaS hardening & Alembic migration rules (Phase 13 — active)

- **Zero friction in local mode.** `KODO_MODE=local` must keep quotas and rate limiting **off** unless
  `KODO_QUOTA_ENABLED` / `KODO_RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED` explicitly enable them, TTL cleanup must never run,
  and deleting a workspace in local mode removes **only the DB bookmark** — never a host folder. Any new
  SaaS-only guard must be inert (or absent) in local mode and covered by a local-mode test.
- **Alembic owns the schema.** Every table/column change needs a migration in
  `services/api-python/alembic/versions/` **and** the matching `app/db/models.py` update. `env.py` reads
  `KODO_DATABASE_URL` at runtime — never hard-code a DSN in `alembic.ini` — and keeps
  `render_as_batch` on SQLite. Revisions are `NNNN_slug.py` with a fixed `revision`/`down_revision`
  pair; `0001_initial_schema` is the baseline and must keep matching the Phase 8/10 schema.
- **Legacy databases must upgrade, never crash.** `run_migrations(engine)` keeps its two branches: a
  database with `users` but no `alembic_version` is **stamped** at `0001_initial_schema` before
  `upgrade head`; a fresh database upgrades directly. Additive migrations must be nullable or carry a
  server default so existing rows survive. `tests/test_saas_hardening.py` covers both paths and must
  stay green.
- **Sandbox containment is absolute.** Quota accounting, deletions and TTL cleanup resolve every path
  and require it to be **strictly inside** `<KODO_WORKSPACES_DIR>/<user_id>/`; a symlink is refused,
  never resolved-and-followed. `compute_dir_size_bytes` walks with
  `os.scandir(follow_symlinks=False)`. A row pointing at a local host folder may be forgotten, never
  deleted.
- **Quotas fail loud and actionable.** Pre-clone: `413 WORKSPACE_QUOTA_EXCEEDED` (count cap) /
  `507 WORKSPACE_QUOTA_EXCEEDED` (disk full). Post-clone: delete the clone immediately and return
  `413 CLONE_SIZE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` or `413 WORKSPACE_QUOTA_EXCEEDED`. Messages must tell the user how
  to recover ("delete a workspace in the Workspace Switcher").
- **TTL cleanup is SaaS-only and audited.** It runs only when `KODO_MODE=saas` and
  `KODO_WORKSPACE_TTL_HOURS > 0`, from the cancellable `asyncio` task in the FastAPI lifespan, and
  records a `workspace_ttl_cleanup` audit event per removal. Scan, file read, apply and Git export must
  refresh `last_accessed_at` (`repository.touch_workspace`) so active workspaces are never collected.
- **Rate limiting lives inside the auth gate.** Middleware order is
  `tenant_guard -> CORSMiddleware -> RateLimitMiddleware -> routes`: the limiter runs **after**
  authentication (so it can key by user id) and **inside** CORS (so a `429` stays readable from a
  split-origin dev UI). The instance is `app.state.rate_limiter` (`SlidingWindowRateLimiter`) and must
  stay replaceable/resettable for deterministic tests. Buckets and `KODO_RL_*_PER_MIN` limits belong in
  `app/config.py`; the 429 body is `{ code, message, retryAfterSeconds }` plus `Retry-After` and
  `X-RateLimit-*` headers. Never add a Redis dependency for the single-instance deployment — keep the
  limiter interface swappable instead.
- **TLS/edge config is deployment, not code.** `deploy/caddy/Caddyfile` keeps HSTS, nosniff,
  frame-deny, referrer-policy, the 32 MB body cap and `flush_interval -1` on
  `/api/v1/build/verify/stream`; `docker-compose.saas.yml` is an overlay that never publishes the
  `web` port. `docs/SAAS_DEPLOYMENT.md` is the runbook — keep it in sync with new env vars.
- **Deterministic tests.** `tests/test_saas_hardening.py` is hermetic (temp SQLite files, temp
  sandboxes, reset limiter, no network/Docker); `tests/conftest.py` boots the throwaway DB with
  `KODO_DB_AUTO_MIGRATE=false` for speed, so the migration path is covered explicitly there.

## 4. Filesystem safety

- Every path coming from the client is untrusted. Resolve it, then verify it is inside the requested root
  (`Path.resolve()` + `is_relative_to`) before opening. Reject traversal with HTTP 400. In SaaS mode the
  requested root must additionally resolve inside the caller's `KODO_WORKSPACES_DIR/<user_id>/` sandbox
  (403 `SAAS_PATH_FORBIDDEN`) — see §3h.
- Never follow symlinks out of the root. Never expose absolute filesystem paths to the UI except the
  scanned `rootPath` the user supplied.
- Skip `node_modules`, `.git`, `bin`, `obj`, `target`, `dist`, `.venv`, `venv`, `__pycache__`,
  `.pytest_cache`, `.mypy_cache`, `.ruff_cache`, `*.egg-info` and `site-packages` when walking.
- Enforce file count / file size limits and report them as `WorkspaceIR.warnings`, not exceptions.
- Dry-run patching and apply repeat the containment check per file, reject absolute paths and `..`,
  and enforce the 2 MB cap. Apply additionally re-checks containment on every rollback target.
- Scaffolding resolves **non-existent** targets with `resolve_new_member`: the deepest existing
  ancestor is resolved and must stay inside the root, symlinked folders are rejected, and the final
  component must not exist. Reverse-verify containment after creating parent folders.
- Docker/Git subprocesses use `resolve_root` for `cwd`, require `docker-compose.yml` to live inside
  that root, and only ever write outside the workspace inside Kodo's own app directories
  (`KODO_WORKSPACES_DIR`, `KODO_AUDIT_LOG_PATH`). Never expose those app paths as workspace paths.

## 5. Degradation, never crashing

- The C# analyzer is optional at runtime. If it is offline, the orchestrator MUST still return
  Java/TS/JS/Python results and append a human-readable entry to `WorkspaceIR.warnings`.
- Unparsable files, unknown extensions, and oversized files produce warnings, never 500s.
- AI Copilot failures (missing key, 401/403, 429, timeout, unreachable local server) return the
  structured error codes from §3e; they never block scanning, auditing or the guarded apply path.
- Docker being unavailable (no CLI, no daemon, no compose file) is a reported status with a warning,
  never a 500; scanning, auditing, patching and applying keep working. Audit-log write failures are
  swallowed. A failed `docker compose up/down` returns `success: false` with stderr instead of raising.
- The UI must render a usable empty state when `nodes` is empty.

## 6. Code style

- Strong typing everywhere: Pydantic v2 models (no raw dicts in the IR path), C# records + nullable
  annotations, TypeScript `strict` with no `any` in the IR path.
- Keep analyzers pure: `parse(text, context) -> nodes + references`. No I/O inside parsing functions.
- Deterministic output: stable node ordering (stack, filePath, name) and deduplicated ids/relations so
  scans of the same tree are diffable.
- Files under ~400 lines. One responsibility per module.
- Comments only where the code cannot explain itself.

## 7. Definition of done for any change

- `dotnet build services/analyzer-csharp` succeeds.
- `python -m pytest services/api-python/tests` succeeds (including the Phase 4 generator suite, which
  re-scans generated projects and asserts a 100/100 self-audit, the Phase 8 suite covering
  restart-safe rollback, tenant isolation, SaaS 401/403 gates and mocked GitHub OAuth, and the
  Phase 12 Python suite).
- `python -m ruff check` on changed Python files succeeds.
- `npm run build` in `apps/web` succeeds (type-check + bundle).
- The full flow works end to end: scan a folder -> graph nodes appear -> UML renders -> file opens
  read-only.
- For patching changes: `docker compose up -d --build analyzer-csharp`, verify `GET /health` and
  `POST /patch`, then scan -> edit a field -> **Preview Diff (Dry-Run)** and confirm the proposed
  content preserves hand-written methods/comments.
- For apply changes: run the flow against a **git-initialised** temp project and confirm, in order,
  dry-run -> apply changes the file on disk and preserves business methods, the re-scan reflects the
  new health score, a dirty tree or a non-Git folder is rejected with 409 until overridden, an
  externally modified file yields 409 `STALE_FILE` with **no** file written, and rollback restores the
  original bytes exactly (`git status` clean again).
- For builder changes: generate the 3-service demo in a git-initialised temp folder from the UI,
  review the NEW files in Monaco, apply, confirm the re-scan shows the full
  controller/service/repository/entity graph at 100/100, roll back, and confirm the folder is empty
  again with a clean `git status` (and that an existing path yields 409 `FILE_EXISTS`).
- For AI changes: run `pytest services/api-python/tests/test_ai_copilot.py` with no network, then in
  the UI press **Test Connection** for both DeepSeek Cloud (401 without a key) and a local
  Ollama/vLLM server. Generate a blueprint, run **Fix with AI** on a real finding and **AI Implement**
  on a method, and confirm each result only reaches disk through the Diff Preview Apply button —
  rollback restores the original bytes.
- For Deploy changes: run `pytest services/api-python/tests/test_deploy_studio.py` (Docker CLI and
  GitHub API mocked), then in the UI generate Docker files for a git-initialised temp project, review
  the four files in Monaco, apply, confirm `docker compose config` passes, roll back and confirm the
  files are gone. With Docker running, press **Compose Up (--build)**, verify the container health
  badges and filtered logs, then **Compose Down**. Confirm `~/.kodo/audit.jsonl` gained entries with
  no token or file content in them.
- For builder verification changes: `pytest services/api-python/tests/test_builder_verify.py` must pass
  with no Docker daemon (CLI and LLM mocked), then `docker compose --profile builders build` and a live
  **Verify Build (Sandbox)** run on a generated project must report the streamed log + parsed
  diagnostics, with `--network=none`, while `git status` and every file mtime stay unchanged.
- For Docker/CI changes: `docker compose config` and
  `docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml config` must validate, then
  `docker compose up --build` must report `web`, `api-python` and `analyzer-csharp` healthy with
  `http://localhost:3000` serving the UI and `/health` proxying to FastAPI (Roslyn reachable). Every
  workflow in `.github/workflows/` must be valid YAML with the documented jobs, and all three images
  must build locally (`docker compose build`) as non-root, multi-stage images.
- For System 1 changes: `pytest services/api-python/tests/test_decision_layer.py` must pass with no
  Ollama/laya-serve process (mocked transport), `docker compose --profile ai-local config` must
  validate, and the UI must show **Triage with Laya (System 1)** badges, the **Screen Diff (Laya S1)**
  banner and the **Decisions (ADR)** tab. Kill the local Laya server and confirm triage/screen routes
  still answer 200 with `available: false`, the audit panel and diff preview keep working, and no
  dialogue ever reaches disk without the normal Apply → SHA-256 → rollback path.
- For folder-browser / graph-UX changes: `pytest services/api-python/tests/test_fs_browser.py` and
  `tests/test_mermaid.py` must pass with no Docker daemon and no real host folder (temp roots only).
  In `KODO_MODE=saas` confirm `GET /api/v1/fs/browse` answers `403 FS_BROWSE_DISABLED_IN_SAAS`
  **with and without** a session token and that no Browse button renders. In `local` mode press
  **Browse…**, drill into a monorepo parent, scan a sub-project, then use `Ctrl+K` + Enter, the layer
  pills, **Issues Only** and **Focus 1-Hop** and confirm the UML tab matches the filtered subset
  (including the >60-class guard). Confirm nothing was uploaded/copied (file mtimes unchanged) and
  that `GET /api/v1/fs/browse` never returns a path outside the configured roots.
- For Python changes: `pytest services/api-python/tests/test_python_stack.py` must pass with no
  network, Docker daemon or host toolchain, and `pytest services/api-python/tests` must stay green.
  Scan Kodo's own `services/api-python` (FastAPI) folder and confirm the routers, services,
  repositories and Pydantic DTOs appear with `depends_on`/`references` edges and no parse warnings;
  open a `.py` file, add/rename a field, **Preview Diff (Dry-Run)** and confirm docstrings and
  methods are untouched; apply and roll back to byte-identical content. Generate a `python-fastapi`
  blueprint and confirm the self-scan reports 100/100, then generate the Python Docker artifacts and
  confirm `docker compose config` accepts the interpolated healthcheck.
